    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        RecordType.Description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it lives here rather than being trimmed away; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. Do not paste this back into
        <description>. NOTE ALSO: the comment must sit INSIDE the root element - a comment
        placed ABOVE <RecordType> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error. The 255 cap has been breached THREE
        times in this programme (both Tranche 2 record types, then Disposition_NDA at 266);
        the lengths in this file were MEASURED with an XML parser, not eyeballed.

        THE BUY-SIDE LOI. Parented by Opportunity__c. Five stages, UNCHANGED from what the org
        already runs: Draft -> Prepare/Review -> Sent -> Counter -> Completed. Nothing on the
        acquisition side is added, removed, renamed or re-ordered by this tranche. This record
        type exists to FENCE the existing behaviour, not to alter it.

        WHY A RECORD TYPE AT ALL (decision D6). LOI__c becomes dual-parented in this tranche -
        Opportunity__c for acquisitions, the new Disposition__c lookup for dispositions - and
        the two sides need DIFFERENT stage sets. A buy-side LOI is drafted by DPEG and sent
        OUT; a sell-side LOI ARRIVES from the buyer. One shared restricted picklist cannot
        express both. Record types give per-type value sets and per-type Paths, so neither
        side sees the other's stages. Reusing LOI__c (rather than inventing a disposition
        object) is what inherits Counter_Offer__c, CounterOfferService, lwc/loiCounterOffer and
        the counter history - which is what makes "every round is recorded" achievable at all.

        THE TWO STAGE SETS ARE FULLY DISJOINT, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PROPERTY. No stage
        value appears on both record types. Consequences, all of them relied upon elsewhere:
        every existing flexipage visibility rule keyed on an acquisition stage value is
        SELF-LIMITING to acquisition LOIs with no record-type criterion added, and the same is
        true in reverse for the disposition rules. Contrast NDA__c, whose Sent and Signed ARE
        shared, which is why NDA rules must test Disposition__c or RecordTypeId instead. If a
        future change makes any LOI stage shared, every one of those rules must be revisited.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-14 (Phase 3, observation 5). THE PARAGRAPH DIRECTLY ABOVE, AND THE
        FIVE-STAGE SEQUENCE DESCRIBED THREE PARAGRAPHS ABOVE IT, ARE BOTH NOW FALSE.
        THE LAST SENTENCE OF THE RETRACTED PARAGRAPH IS THE ONE THAT CAME TRUE.
        =====================================================================

        RETRACTED (1), verbatim: "Five stages, UNCHANGED from what the org already runs: Draft ->
        Prepare/Review -> Sent -> Counter -> Completed. Nothing on the acquisition side is added,
        removed, renamed or re-ordered by this tranche. This record type exists to FENCE the
        existing behaviour, not to alter it."
        The buy-side sequence is now Draft -> Under Review -> Submitted -> Negotiation -> Signed.
        Prepare/Review, Sent, Counter and Completed are RETIRED (deactivated at Phase 3 D3 after
        a row migration, never deleted - decision O3). The fencing statement was true of Tranche
        3B and is not a standing property of this file.

        RETRACTED (2), verbatim: "THE TWO STAGE SETS ARE FULLY DISJOINT... every existing
        flexipage visibility rule keyed on an acquisition stage value is SELF-LIMITING to
        acquisition LOIs with no record-type criterion added."
        'Under Review' is now carried by BOTH record types. It was REUSED, not added - it has
        been on Disposition_LOI since 3B - which is precisely why the overlap is invisible in the
        value-set diff for this file and shows up only when both files are read together.

        🔴 SO THE RETRACTED PARAGRAPH'S OWN CLOSING CONDITION HAS FIRED: "If a future change
        makes any LOI stage shared, every one of those rules must be revisited." It did, and they
        were. What is NOT true is that the fix was mechanical - see the residual below.

        WHAT SURVIVES THE RETRACTION, AND IT MATTERS: THE TWO TERMINALS ARE STILL DISTINCT.
        'Signed' is acquisition-only and 'Executed' is disposition-only, so a terminal-state test
        can still be written by naming values - which is what keeps Is_Advance_Allowed__c's
        terminal clauses working unchanged. It is the MIDDLE of the sequence that is now
        ambiguous, and exactly one live rule depends on it: the approval gate. An acquisition LOI
        at 'Under Review' is awaiting approval; a disposition LOI at 'Under Review' is being read
        by DPEG and has no approval concept at all. Is_Advance_Allowed__c therefore now carries a
        RecordType.DeveloperName test INSIDE that one disjunction and nowhere else; its own
        header records the instruction it retracts to do so.

        ⚠ RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN CLOSED - the Submit for Approval button.
        flexipages/LOI_Record_Page gates LOI__c.Submit_for_Approval on
        {!Record.Stage__c} EQUAL 'Prepare/Review', which becomes 'Under Review' - a SHARED value.
        A flexipage visibility rule cannot express a record-type test directly (recorded in
        NDA__c.Is_Decline_Allowed__c's header), so that button is no longer self-limiting and an
        acquisition driver opening a DISPOSITION LOI at 'Under Review' will see it. It is the
        same residual this page already names and accepts for Advance Stage. It was NOT closed
        here because the only in-repo mechanism for closing it - a new formula-checkbox
        discriminator - would make ONE currently-working acquisition button newly dependent on
        a brand-new field's FLS, which is the same class of trade Is_Decline_Allowed__c's header
        refuses to make. The named remedy is in the Phase 3 runbook.

        ⚠ THE COST FIGURE ABOVE READ "three ... buttons" UNTIL 2026-08-14 AND WAS WRONG BY A
        FACTOR OF THREE. It came from the build brief, which assumed all FOUR acquisition
        visibility rules on LOI_Record_Page would need a record-type criterion. The runbook's own
        analysis (1.6) then established that only ONE gate becomes shared - Submit_for_Approval,
        whose criterion moves 'Prepare/Review' -> 'Under Review'. Mark_Countered and
        Mark_Completed move 'Sent' -> 'Submitted', both acquisition-only, so they stay
        self-limiting; the acquisition Advance_Stage entry carries no stage criterion at all
        (its record-type question moved into Is_Advance_Allowed__c's formula). A discriminator
        would therefore be added to exactly one rule. The overruled figure was then quoted
        forward into this file; it is corrected here and in the runbook, and gate G7 is now given
        the real number, because a decision that turns on cost must be handed the true cost.
        ⚠ THE DECISION IS UNCHANGED, and the smaller number does not reopen it: one button newly
        dependent on a brand-new field's FLS is still an instance of the retired *_Driver__c
        defect class - a rule referencing a field the user cannot read evaluates FALSE with no
        error and no log.
        ⚠ ALSO NOTE the premise: "a flexipage visibility rule cannot express a record-type test
        directly" is REPO-ASSERTED AND UNMEASURED. A repo-wide sweep returns zero
        {!Record.RecordType.*} criteria, but nobody has tried one in this org and been refused.
        Runbook remedy (a) is to try it, and it falsifies the claim either way.

        ⚠ ORDERING WAS RE-CHECKED, NOT INHERITED. See Stage__c.field-meta.xml's own comment: an
        APPEND is sufficient for this record type only because the one shared value happens to
        sit at a position correct for both sequences. Contract_Review__c in this same phase
        needed an INSERT. Redo the arithmetic; do not carry the answer across.

        STEP ORDER IS ENFORCED BY THE MASTER VALUE SET, NOT BY THE PATH FILE. Runtime display
        order comes from Stage__c's picklist value order (sorted=false), filtered by this
        record type - so a record type can only include or exclude, never re-order. The five
        disposition values were therefore APPENDED after the five acquisition values, which
        keeps this record type's rendering byte-identical to today's LOI_Path AND yields the
        correct disposition sequence. Because the sets are disjoint, appending is sufficient
        here; the NDA field needed an interleave (Not Sent had to be moved ahead of Sent)
        precisely because its sets overlap. Anyone re-ordering Stage__c must re-check BOTH
        record type files.

        WARNING - RECORD-TYPE PICKLIST RESTRICTION IS UI-ONLY. It is NOT enforced by Apex DML,
        so restricting Received/Under Review/Countered by DPEG/Counter Received from
        Buyer/Executed out here does not stop a direct write. The Apex-side allow-list in
        RecordStageAdvanceService must become record-type-aware in the same tranche; that is
        the parallel developer change and it is what actually refuses
        advanceTo(acquisitionLoiId, 'Executed').

        THE APPROVAL PATH BELONGS TO THIS RECORD TYPE ALONE, AND THAT IS ALREADY STRUCTURAL.
        Opportunity.LOI_Approval is an approval on the OPPORTUNITY object with entryCriteria
        StageName equals LOI - it has no relationship to an LOI__c record and cannot fire on
        one. The LOI__c.Submit_for_Approval quick action is gated on
        {!Record.Stage__c} EQUAL 'Prepare/Review', a value the Disposition_LOI record type does
        not carry, so it is self-limiting by the disjoint value set with no record-type
        criterion needed. Submitted_Date__c, Approved_By__c, Approved_Date__c and
        Approval_Comments__c are LAYOUT-EXCLUDED from the disposition type, NOT deleted -
        acquisition rows still hold their values.

        Prepare%2FReview IS URL-ENCODED ON PURPOSE. Inside a RecordType, a PicklistValue
        fullName carrying a special character must be encoded; the same value is stored
        LITERALLY as Prepare/Review in Stage__c.field-meta.xml and in the PathAssistant. The
        repo precedent is Dead%2FPass in objects/Opportunity/businessProcesses/*. Do not
        "correct" either form to match the other.

        EVERY PICKLIST ON THE OBJECT IS ENUMERATED - Stage__c, LOI_Status__c and
        Ball_In_Court__c - because a record type file that omits a picklist silently drops all
        of its values from that type. Precedent:
        objects/Opportunity/recordTypes/Commercial.recordType-meta.xml, which enumerates nine.
        LOI__c has exactly three picklists; the fields/ directory was walked to establish that.

        LOI_Status__c AND Ball_In_Court__c ARE ENUMERATED IN FULL AND IDENTICALLY ON BOTH
        RECORD TYPES, DELIBERATELY. LOI_Status__c is acquisition vocabulary and is excluded
        from the disposition LAYOUT, but its Draft default must remain available on both types
        because Is_Advance_Allowed__c reads it on every LOI. Ball_In_Court__c is left at
        Us/Seller pending an explicit decision - on a sale 'Seller' would mean DPEG, which
        INVERTS the field's meaning; that inversion is raised as a recommendation and is
        deliberately NOT resolved by this file.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-10 (code review 3B/3C, W1). THE Ball_In_Court__c SENTENCES IN THE
        PARAGRAPH DIRECTLY ABOVE ARE FALSE. THE LOI_Status__c SENTENCE STANDS.
        =====================================================================

        RETRACTED, verbatim: "LOI_Status__c AND Ball_In_Court__c ARE ENUMERATED IN FULL AND
        IDENTICALLY ON BOTH RECORD TYPES... Ball_In_Court__c is left at Us/Seller pending an
        explicit decision... that inversion is raised as a recommendation and is DELIBERATELY
        NOT RESOLVED BY THIS FILE."

        THREE THINGS IN THAT SENTENCE ARE WRONG, AND THE FIRST IS THE ONE THAT MISLEADS:
          1. Ball_In_Court__c IS **NOT** IDENTICAL ON BOTH RECORD TYPES, AND MUST NOT BE MADE SO.
             This type carries {Us, Seller}; Disposition_LOI carries {Us, Buyer} - it added
             'Buyer' in 3B and had 'Seller' restricted out on 2026-08-10 under review W2. The two
             sets are now deliberately DIFFERENT, and that difference IS the inversion fix. A
             reader who "restored consistency" here by adding 'Buyer' to this type would put the
             value on the ACQUISITION side, where DPEG *is* the buyer, so it would name DPEG -
             the exact inversion the split exists to prevent, mirrored.
          2. THE INVERSION WAS RESOLVED, not merely recommended. CounterOfferService.isSaleSide
             derives the label from the parent and stores the derived value, never the caller's
             token, and it REFUSES 'Buyer' on an acquisition parent - which is the Apex half that
             makes this type's {Us, Seller} restriction actually enforceable, since record-type
             picklist restriction is UI-ONLY.
          3. IT WAS NOT RESOLVED "BY THIS FILE" EITHER - that part was never the claim's problem.
             The resolution is Disposition_LOI plus CounterOfferService. THIS file's contribution
             is the negative one: excluding 'Buyer' so an acquisition LOI cannot carry it.

        ⚠ THE LOI_Status__c HALF OF THE RETRACTED PARAGRAPH IS UNAFFECTED AND STILL CORRECT.
        LOI_Status__c genuinely IS enumerated in full and identically on both record types, for
        the reason given: Is_Advance_Allowed__c reads it on every LOI and its Draft default must
        stay available on both, or the formula's acquisition-only terms stop being inert on a
        disposition row. Do not let this retraction take that sentence with it.

        No <businessProcess> - that element is for Opportunity, Case, Lead and Solution only.

        POST-DEPLOY, NOT DEPLOYABLE: this record type must be assigned in every profile in use
        (System Administrator included - Modify All Data is an OBJECT permission and confers no
        record-type access, and the LOI block of OpportunityReviewService runs as the APPROVER,
        so the approver profiles must be assigned too), a per-profile DEFAULT must be chosen,
        the disposition layout must be assigned to the disposition type, and every existing
        LOI row must be migrated off Master to Acquisition_LOI (every LOI in the org today is
        acquisition-side by construction - LOI__c had no Disposition__c lookup before this
        tranche). Read RecordTypeId BACK afterwards; a script that ran without error is not
        evidence the type landed. profiles/** is .forceignore'd in this repo.
